WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.540
Alicia Arbaje: "It is inevitable

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.539
that you will get dementia."

00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:05.403
No, no, and no.

00:00:05.403 --> 00:00:08.700
Ebi Ofrey: "Never smiling
staves off wrinkles."

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.960
Wrinkles are not as a result of smiling.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.849
Arbaje: Even if it is, do we
really want to stop smiling?

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.630
"You don't need as much
sleep when you get older."

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.300
You always need sleep.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.759
Hi, my name is Dr. Alicia Arbaje.

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:25.000
Ofrey: And my name is Dr. Ebi Ofrey.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.933
Arbaje: And today, we're
going to be debunking

00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:28.210
myths about aging.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.330
"It's harder to learn new
things as you get older."

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.890
It may be a little slower on some things,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.951
especially if we're
not familiar with them,

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:37.342
but as we get older,

00:00:37.342 --> 00:00:40.067
we become more able to understand things

00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.640
that are more abstract
as opposed to concrete.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.095
So I think this bodes
well for older adults.

00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:48.200
Ofrey: As Dr. Arbaje said,
I think it's just different.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.470
Older adults are more likely
to learn things faster

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.690
that don't have to do with technology.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.690
But once technology comes into play,

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.298
it takes them a bit longer.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.864
"How you age is
predetermined by your genes."

00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:07.050
Arbaje: This is one of my
favorite ones to talk about.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.380
I think that genes are way
less important than we think

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.920
and lifestyle is way more
important than we think.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.590
And we have the ability
to turn genes on and off;

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.431
how we eat, sleep, live, love,

00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:25.270
and exercise makes the
biggest difference of all.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.958
Ofrey: So, your habits from youth

00:01:26.958 --> 00:01:30.400
play a key role in how
your aging process goes.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.442
Arbaje: "It is inevitable
that you will get dementia."

00:01:33.442 --> 00:01:35.310
No, no, and no.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.960
Dementia is not a normal part of aging,

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.960
and it's important to
understand that if you're having

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.799
memory problems or problems planning

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:46.070
or being oriented or getting around,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.630
that is a sign that something
needs to be checked out.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.390
Ofrey: Only a small
percentage of older adults

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.240
actually have dementia.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.420
So if you have problems
with remembering things,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.770
you need to understand that
it could also be due to

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.297
an underlying problem that can be solved.

00:02:01.297 --> 00:02:04.740
"You shouldn't exercise when you are older

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.290
because you are prone to injuries."

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.823
If you have underlying conditions

00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:10.998
you are aware of, it's important

00:02:10.998 --> 00:02:13.600
you pay attention to the exercises you do

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.024
because your underlying condition

00:02:15.024 --> 00:02:17.660
might affect your risk of injury.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.320
But old age alone is
not a risk for injury.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.753
Arbaje: I wholeheartedly
agree and would add that

00:02:24.753 --> 00:02:27.024
there are different kinds of exercises

00:02:27.024 --> 00:02:29.480
no matter what situation you are in.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.110
You could be bedbound or in
a chair or in a wheelchair

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.380
and still be able to do
different kinds of exercises.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.220
"You don't need as much
sleep when you get older."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.008
You always need sleep.

00:02:41.008 --> 00:02:43.368
And the amount of sleep you need over time

00:02:43.368 --> 00:02:45.750
is probably gonna be pretty consistent.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.450
And the way to know if
you're getting enough or not

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.700
is to understand how well
you're doing the next morning.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.700
Are you able to function the next day

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.500
and do the things that you have to do?

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.595
Ofrey: As we go into sort of retirement,

00:02:57.595 --> 00:02:59.316
most of the time we have more time

00:02:59.316 --> 00:03:01.445
available to us during the day,

00:03:01.445 --> 00:03:03.920
and many times we take
more naps than we should.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.942
As such, we should be able
to adjust our daily habits

00:03:06.942 --> 00:03:09.780
to ensure we don't spend
most of the day sleeping

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.740
and then not be able to sleep at night.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:16.566
"You'll gain weight because
your metabolism slows down."

00:03:16.566 --> 00:03:19.140
I feel there's a chance of weight gain

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.720
not because your metabolism slows down,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.320
but because you are more
inactive at the time.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.350
We need to still remain
active the older we get.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.345
Arbaje: Your metabolism should also stay

00:03:29.345 --> 00:03:32.021
pretty consistent throughout our lives.

00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:35.053
So, I really think that
this can empower people

00:03:35.053 --> 00:03:37.270
to not feel like old age is going

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.253
to be about a lot of decline.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.648
The next myth is that,

00:03:41.648 --> 00:03:44.460
"People lose their sex
drives as they age."

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.440
So, this one's kind of interesting.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.450
If people are losing
their sex drive, per se,

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:53.310
it could be because of an
underlying chronic situation,

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.750
a chronic condition, it could
be related to medications.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.690
People's relationships change over time,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.620
and what becomes important to them

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.980
may change or evolve over time.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.177
It doesn't necessarily mean that

00:04:06.177 --> 00:04:07.610
they wouldn't have a sex drive.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.160
Ofrey: I totally agree with Dr. Arbaje.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.388
The sense that people may
have different interests

00:04:13.388 --> 00:04:16.900
and different ways of showing
affection the older they get.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.192
And, again, there may
be underlying conditions

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:21.145
that may make sexual intercourse

00:04:21.145 --> 00:04:23.920
not as comfortable as it used to be.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.576
So, "Getting old makes you grouchy."

00:04:27.576 --> 00:04:29.840
Arbaje: We see how older
people are depicted

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.965
in cartoons and in movies,

00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:34.180
and often this is the stereotype.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.905
Grumpy Old Man: I don't like this desk,

00:04:35.905 --> 00:04:37.300
and I don't like this chair,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.442
and I don't like holidays,

00:04:39.442 --> 00:04:42.420
flippity floppity floopity floody!

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.770
Ofrey: We realize that a
majority of our patients

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.881
are not grouchy, but
they seem to be a bit sad

00:04:47.881 --> 00:04:50.683
because of the, you know,
the nesting syndrome,

00:04:50.683 --> 00:04:53.351
being alone in the house,
and things like that.

00:04:53.351 --> 00:04:56.570
But grouchiness is not
a feature of old age.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:57.930
Arbaje: That is so well said.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.050
Grouchiness is not a feature of old age.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.600
I do think that

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.633
depression is a lot
higher in older adults,

00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:06.690
or it can be,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.398
and that can be because of

00:05:08.398 --> 00:05:11.481
underlying chronic
conditions, life circumstance,

00:05:11.481 --> 00:05:13.480
and those are things that depression

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.130
can manifest as irritability
in older people,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.507
and that may be mistaken for grouchiness.

00:05:20.507 --> 00:05:22.230
"You will lose your eyesight."

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.179
Losing eyesight is usually

00:05:24.179 --> 00:05:27.950
related to a problem in
other organs as well.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.330
So it's a sign of an underlying condition.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.328
Poor circulation or diabetes

00:05:32.328 --> 00:05:34.090
or high blood pressure or strokes.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.623
Now, what we do know

00:05:35.623 --> 00:05:38.170
is that the acuity of your vision

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:39.810
can change over time.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.910
So, the sharpness of your vision,

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.280
but that is not the same
as losing your eyesight.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.105
Ofrey: You may have
certain visual impairments,

00:05:48.105 --> 00:05:50.200
but not loss of eyesight.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:53.130
And all those impairments
have corrective measures

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:56.560
that can be applied to
ensure your sight returns,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.261
if not to normal, to almost normal.

00:05:59.261 --> 00:06:03.502
So, "Your teeth fall
out as you get older."

00:06:03.502 --> 00:06:06.314
Again, there's a popular
saying over here meaning,

00:06:06.314 --> 00:06:09.140
"Don't prepare for war in time of war."

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:12.260
Meaning you prepare for
old age before you get old.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.813
Most of the time, people
take care of their bodies

00:06:14.813 --> 00:06:16.890
but seem to neglect oral health.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.377
And that's why it seems that

00:06:18.377 --> 00:06:20.220
most times people lose their teeth.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.010
Arbaje: Your teeth are so important

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:24.520
because all those bacteria
travel to other parts

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.150
of our body, so it's
really important to keep,

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.497
as I said earlier, all
our organs in good shape,

00:06:30.497 --> 00:06:33.600
and that will help our teeth
stay in good shape as well.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.230
"Aging means more medications."

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:40.030
So, this one is
unfortunately partially true,

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.800
but it doesn't have to be.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.950
It does seem that the older people get

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:46.890
and the more healthcare they receive,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.400
the more medications they might end up on.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.460
The theme here is how you eat,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:53.880
how you sleep, how you
exercise, how you love,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.240
how you pray. All of these things matter.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.801
Even if you are on medications,

00:06:58.801 --> 00:07:02.370
there is still a chance you
can come off of medications

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.950
with lifestyle changes.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:05.930
So, anyone who's on a medication,

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:10.310
I just encourage them to see
their healthcare provider,

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:14.187
geriatrician, ideally, and
ask several times a year,

00:07:14.187 --> 00:07:15.990
"Do I still need this medication?

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.092
And if I do, do I still
need it at the same dose?

00:07:19.092 --> 00:07:22.810
And if I still need it, what
else can I do in my life

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.370
so that I can eventually come off

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.560
of this medication if possible?"

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.380
Ofrey: Interestingly, I
was thinking the same thing

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.837
that Arbaje was thinking.

00:07:29.837 --> 00:07:32.557
And so it's important to note that

00:07:32.557 --> 00:07:35.200
if you have a healthy lifestyle,

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.560
you are less likely to
require most of the medication

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.710
many elderly people take at the moment

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.960
because you have prevented the conditions

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.280
that require them.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:47.850
"Never smiling staves off wrinkles."

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:52.160
Wrinkles are not as a result of smiling.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.722
Arbaje: Even if it is, do we
really want to stop smiling?

00:07:54.722 --> 00:07:57.070
Because smiling has so
many other benefits.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.060
Wrinkles are about loss of collagen,

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.380
loss of hydration, and not so much

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.347
how you're using your face.

00:08:03.347 --> 00:08:05.120
So please don't stop smiling.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:07.860
And please instead hydrate if
you're worried about wrinkles,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.293
make sure you're eating plenty
of fruits and vegetables.

00:08:10.293 --> 00:08:12.581
Really, skin is an inside job.

00:08:12.581 --> 00:08:15.182
"Eating fewer calories
will extend your life."

00:08:15.182 --> 00:08:18.460
What we know is the
kind of calories you eat

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:20.570
are more important than how many.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.320
So, our bodies run on glucose,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:26.186
and the more pure sources of glucose

00:08:26.186 --> 00:08:28.260
you can consume, the better.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.006
Fruit has the most viable available

00:08:31.006 --> 00:08:34.205
source of glucose available to our bodies.

00:08:34.205 --> 00:08:36.770
And when I say eat way
more fruits and vegetables,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:40.031
I mean way more.

00:08:40.031 --> 00:08:43.300
Four apples, three mangoes, six potatoes.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.157
Like, not this tiny-side-salad
stuff that we think about.

00:08:47.157 --> 00:08:48.743
Make that the majority of your meal.

00:08:48.743 --> 00:08:52.500
It is hard to get fresh
produce all year round,

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.920
depending on where you are.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.580
And it can be more
expensive for some people.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.680
I am not saying that it's
important to eat organic,

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.010
although if you can, that's even better,

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:05.060
but I don't want people
to shun getting access

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.930
to whatever kinds of fruit
and vegetable they have.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:10.533
Ofrey: Preparation is the key to success.

00:09:10.533 --> 00:09:13.907
So your lifestyle, what you do,

00:09:13.907 --> 00:09:17.087
your habits you form before you get older,

00:09:17.087 --> 00:09:18.836
they'll go a long way to ensuring

00:09:18.836 --> 00:09:21.420
you have a healthier and longer life.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.853
Arbaje: Adjust your attitude about aging.

00:09:23.853 --> 00:09:27.045
We have this idea that
aging is about decline

00:09:27.045 --> 00:09:29.270
and being dependent on other people.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:31.530
Aging is a beautiful part of life.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:33.500
It's a time when we become more wise,

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:36.119
when we become able to
give back to others,

00:09:36.119 --> 00:09:39.233
and we as geriatricians
are here to support you.

